<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Karakalpakstan</h1><p class="st-meta">1925 CE – present · capital Nukus</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The autonomous republic of the Karakalpak people on the lower Amu Darya, now part of Uzbekistan and at the heart of the Aral Sea disaster.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakalpaks">Karakalpaks</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchak-languages">Kipchak Turkic</a> people living in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> delta under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/khanate-of-khiva">Khanate of Khiva</a>, received their own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakalpak-autonomous-oblast">Karakalpak Autonomous Oblast</a> in 1925, created from lands of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/khorezm-peoples-soviet-republic">Khorezm People's Soviet Republic</a> and attached to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kazakh-assr">Kazakh ASSR</a>. In 1932 it became the Karakalpak ASSR within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and in 1936 it was transferred to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uzbek-ssr">Uzbek SSR</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its capital moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turtkul">Turtkul</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nukus">Nukus</a> in 1939. The economy was based on irrigated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, along with fishing on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aral-sea">Aral Sea</a>. Massive Soviet irrigation from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> caused the sea to shrink from the 1960s, destroying the fishing industry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moynaq">Moynaq</a> and bringing salt storms, polluted water and high rates of disease. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savitsky-museum">Savitsky museum</a> in Nukus preserved banned Soviet avant-garde art.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Karakalpakstan declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in 1990 and joined independent <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> in 1993 as a sovereign republic with a constitutional right to secede. In 2022 proposals to remove that right sparked deadly protests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nukus">Nukus</a>, and the plan was withdrawn.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/karakalpakstan.jpg" alt="Karakalpakstan" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Urmas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Karakalpakstan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1925 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nukus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Karakalpak, Uzbek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Karakalpak ASSR, Republic of Karakalpakstan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1976&amp;focus=karakalpakstan" aria-label="Karakalpakstan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6124 668 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Karakalpakstan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6444 924L6853 898M6444 924L5820 533M6444 924L6691 960M6444 924L6691 960"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6853 898h0M5820 533h0M6691 960h0M6691 960h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6444 924h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6444 924h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nukus. <a href="/atlas?year=1976&amp;focus=karakalpakstan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

